and regulate their market  which would in turn require testing to  be done in dc itself  so overall we have 11 states moving  towards legalizing cannabis for medical  use and we have 10 states moving towards  legalizing cannabis for recreational use  six of the states that are looking to  have it legalized medically  would have testing required in their  legislation so we can see  a continuing trend of the majority of  states moving forth to legalize in some  fashion  are looking to also require testing to  ensure compliance and to ensure that the  consumers  know what's in the product that they're  purchasing  so we also have some states that are  looking to add testing requirements  later in 2020  so arizona is a state that hasn't been  requiring testing historically but they  are moving towards  actually requiring it for everything  that's going to be in the market  so as of november 1st 2020 all products  in the state of arizona will need to be  tested by an accredited laboratory  obama is another one of those states so  obviously they legalized medicine not  too terribly long ago uh and there are  testing labs there that are accredited  that are fully operational  but as of april 1st 2020 they will  actually be required to have everything  tested  whereas now it's more on a voluntary  basis and laboratories are up and ready  to go ready to take on what we're there  so more on the testing regulation side  so i think it's pretty cool how the  cannabis industry  has really responded to different issues  that have come up in different instances  we've seen a lot of states revising  their testing requirements in the past  couple of years  to require additional testing and a  response potentially to  something going on in the market or a  recall so it's been pretty interesting  compared to other  industries with testing legislators have  been really looking to  respond to public outcry in certain  situations  so for example you see maryland and  michigan among  several other states are requiring  vitamin e acetate testing  so although we could get into the topic  of vaping and by many acetate  i'll just leave it brief and say that  although most of it's coming  from black market products there are  some states that have issued recalls for  vitamin e acetate being  and the final product on the shelves of  dispensaries so we're seeing more states  in addition to michigan and maryland  also required by mini acetate testing  colorado has also been adding more  requirements they've added heavy metal  requirements pesticide requirements  in addition to many others and  california many of you guys are probably  familiar with but they expanded their  testing panel  of required tests three times in 2018  and another thing i'd like to touch on  is more states are also realizing the  importance of microbiological testing  just to make sure there's not certain  things present in the final product on  the shelves  that's been really important as well so  in my opinion compared to other  industries it's holly works with we've  definitely seen  people respond and add additional  testing when they feel like it's  necessary  and that can be a challenge for  laboratories but it's also  their way of ensuring that the public  feels like they can be more confident in  the final product that they're  purchasing  so i just want to touch really quick on  the usda's proposed testing requirements  i know most of you guys are probably  very aware of it so  i'm not going to necessarily go into a  ton of detail but i did want to touch on  a few things that they're proposing  as well as some of the comments that  we've seen that a lot of people  are working towards so currently  they strongly recommend iso iec 17025  accreditation by accreditation body  that's part of the ilac mra  so someone like an h2oi so they do say  strongly recommend they don't say  required as of yet  but based on how things are going which  i'll touch on my next slide it's likely  that that would be required  probably one of the hottest topics in  this hemp bill  is requiring de registration of testing  laboratories  so currently there's very few home  testing labs when you compare to the  rest of  the bonds in existence that are actually  registered with the dea currently as at  the time of this presentation it's 44  hemp testing laboratories  and when you compare all the other  laboratories that are up and running and  accredited  or not yet accredited and just getting  set up that's not a huge  portion of the labs in existence  um i've never personally worked with the  dea at hpla but i have some of my  customers who have and it sounds like  the process is quite  tedious and also at the same time  getting a drug enforcement agency  involved with testing a product that's  supposed to  now be federally legal a lot of  laboratories have seen that as something  that necessarily shouldn't be required  because that's still treating the plant  like it is  an irregular substance it also leaves  the legal limit of thc at 0.3  so we've seen some farmers somehow  growers  manufacturers in seats come forward and  say that  they want a little more leniency on this  point three percent that  no one seems to know where this point  three percent actually originated from  so that's another thing that they put  they put the heart and fast point three  there's some states that have left their  legal limit up a little bit higher and a  lot a little more leeway but that's  really rare and it's kind of unclear  if the usda is setting this at the 0.3  will actually  mean that other states have to often  completely fall within that  so the usda received almost 5 000  comments on their  regulation their interim rule perhaps uh  4  646 comments to be exact were received  by the usda  there are some trends that um holi and  our government relations team  noticed throughout these comments and i  did want to touch on some of this  so requiring iso iec 175 accreditation  instead of just strongly um recommending  it  is a comment that a lot of states a lot  of industry groups and a lot of testing  labs themselves actually put forward  it's an iso standard that's been around  for a really long time  decades and at this point it's used in a  lot of other industries as the standard  and the bar that is set for food  industry environmental industry  electrical industries mechanical  industries calibration testing  laboratories  so other industries do rely on this iso  standard  so it is something that a lot of people  even like some farm groups um  states themselves like i said are trying  to promote this because a lot of  laboratories in the state already doing  this  so i reinvent the wheel when the states  are already happy with what's going on  another thing is that  they're looking to require sampling by a  third party  so a lot of states  um have their own sampling requirements  for hemp  or for canvas right now where they  either require the testing laboratory to  do the sampling or they require the  grower themselves  to do the sampling obviously there's  some impartiality and some risk issues  when it comes to a grower manufacturer  sampling from their own product  so a lot of the comments that went  through are actually pushing for the  laboratory or a third party to do all  the sampling  just to ensure that there's no bias and  there's um impartiality when it comes to  the sampling  i think one of the biggest things that  it sounds like has been a trend  is not requiring dea registration for  some reasons i  previously touched on um having  laboratories jump through groups to work  with another federal agency is something  that people aren't really working  towards  and people even on the growers side  manufacturer side are worried  that having this step be done and then  meeting their product or their plant to  be tested it's going to create  a pretty big backlog when you look at  there's currently 44 dea labs and  knowing that they do have to jump  through some boots to get that  lastly like i mentioned before they want  the delta 9 th levels to be above the  0.3  if at all possible because the amount of  money lost  on a whole crop or a yield if the  percentage comes back from the testing  laboratories higher than that  can be extremely significant so people  are looking to have a little bit more  leeway  on that percentage it's unclear if they  will move forward with this  but that is another thing that we're  seeing trending in the comments  in terms of testing and the acceptable  legal limit there  so that's it for my presentation here's  my contact information if you guys  need it or have any questions for me i'd  be happy to  answer that and feel free to reach out  to me at any point in time  um again thank you so much for listening  to my presentation today and i look  forward to hearing from you if you have  any questions  thank you


